lesson physics-what-changes-a-swing v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=1 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What changes a swing?"
  goal "Time a pendulum fairly and show that only the length of its string changes how long a swing takes."

  look {
    scene 36x16 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        pendulum short length=3 mass=50 angle=10 tag="A"
        pendulum long length=7 mass=200 angle=20 tag="B"
        stopwatch w seconds=17
      }
    }
    say """
      The mill in the canal town keeps its time with a pendulum. A pendulum takes the same time for
      every swing, so a clockmaker times ten swings and divides by ten, which shares out the mistake
      a thumb makes on the stopwatch. Three things could change the time of a swing: the length of
      the string, the weight of the bob and how far out it is let go. To find out which, change one
      of them and keep the other two the same. Only the string's length matters: a longer string
      swings more slowly.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked physics.swing-one-swing l=5 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.swing-one-swing count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=4
    practice physics.swing-why-ten count=1 seed=1
    practice physics.swing-mean count=1 seed=2 hard-count=2
    practice physics.swing-fair count=2 seed=4 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=4
    practice physics.swing-predict count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=4
  }
  story {
    show physics.canal-rope-swing secs=28 dad=80 easy-dad=50 hard-secs=32
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.swing-clock u=0 hard-u=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.swing-length-for-time v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Only the length of the string changes how long a swing takes: longer is slower."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Most children, and many adults, expect a heavier bob to swing faster, and some expect a swing
    let go from further out to take longer. The lesson tests each idea with a fair test, where one
    thing changes and the other two are kept the same, and the answer is the same both times: only
    the string matters. Watch the fair-test questions closely: a child who says yes whenever the
    thing being tested has changed has not yet looked for the second change. If the timing questions
    stall on the decimals, work one with a real stopwatch, then stay here another day. Off the page,
    tie a key to a piece of string, count ten swings against a phone's stopwatch three times, take
    the mean, then halve the string and do it again. The three-star question has no method given:
    the pattern is that four times the length takes twice the time. In the rope swing question a
    rider is not a small bob on a string, so a heavier rider changes the time a little, but by far
    less than a stopwatch in a hand can catch.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the strings are short and a worked example comes first, one fair-test question has only the thing tested changing, the story's uncle weighs 50 kg, and the three-star string is found for 20 seconds."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the strings are long, the mean is asked twice, the fair-test questions each hide a second change, the clock to mend is fast rather than slow, and the three-star question asks for 28 seconds, a length the table's pattern has to be carried well past."
  }
}
